Policy on Coupon Purchased for Research Projects or Activity Participants
1          General Principles
1.1       This  policy  outlines  the  principles  and  procedures  for  the  purchase,  distribution,  and management  of  coupons  (including  gift  cards,  vouchers,   and  digital  codes)  used  to compensate or incentivize individuals participating in University-approved research projects or  sponsored  activities.     Sponsored   activities   include   educational   events,  seminars, conferences, student campaigns and other activities as approved by the respective Dean/Head of Department.
1.2       This  policy  applies  to  all  University  staff and  faculty  involved  in  research  or  activity coordination where coupons are used as part of participant engagement or compensation.
1.3       Coupons include physical or electronic gift cards, vouchers or digital codes with monetary value.  Participants are individual who take part in research studies or University-sponsored activities.

2          Coupon Purchase and Approval
2.1       No  coupons  or  e-coupons  shall  be  purchased  until  the  Principal  Investigator  (PI)  or responsible  department  has  confirmed  and  documented  that  they  are  allowable  and appropriate for the specific project or activity under the designated funding source. All such purchases must be charged to non-UGC funds, unless expressly permitted by the funding source.
2.2       The PI or department must ensure that sufficient budget is available and obtain prior approval from the respective Dean/Head of Department or their delegates. The project / activity plan (including but not limited to the below information) shall also be provided to facilitate their approval.
•   Project/activity
•   PI/department
•   Purpose/justification for using coupons
•   Budget availability, funding source and fund code
•   Coupon details: type/format, value per coupon, quantity, total value
•   Timing and distribution plan
•   Entitlement rules: who is eligible, limits per person (number/value/frequency), staff / student eligibility, verification method
•   Contact person and custodian of the coupons
2.3       For human-subjects research, the coupon arrangement and participant involvement must be clearly described in the research application and receive prior approval from the relevant funding authority before any incentives or coupons are purchased.
2.4       Personal purchases for later reimbursement are not permitted. To secure the best pricing through centralized, bulk buying, all coupon and e-coupon purchases must be processed by the Finance Office (FO) Procurement Team.
2.5       Purchase    coupons    redeemable    primarily    for    alcohol,     cannabis,    vaping/tobacco, firearms/ammunition,  adult  entertainment,  gambling,  or  other  categories  posing  legal, ethical, or reputational risk are not allowed.




2.6       When selecting coupons or e-coupons, departments must consider the timeline of the project or activity to ensure that recipients have sufficient time to redeem them. Coupons with short expiration periods or those that incur inactivity fees should be avoided to prevent loss of value and ensure participant benefit.

3          Coupon Distribution
3.1       Coupons are distributed only to eligible participants as per the approved project or activity plan.
3.2       A detailed distribution log (Annex  1) must be maintained, including recipient identifiers (e.g., participant ID), date of distribution, coupon type and value, serial number and recipient acknowledgment.
3.3       For e-coupons,  secure and verifiable delivery methods (e.g., encrypted email or secured platforms) must be used to protect participant privacy and prevent misuse.
3.4       Physical coupons must be stored securely prior to distribution and handled by authorized personnel only.
3.5       For research projects, the Principal Investigator (PI) must report the quantity and value of any  unused  coupons  to  the  relevant   funding  authority  and  obtain  agreement  on  the appropriate disposition. Any unused or returned coupons must be documented and submitted to the FO for reconciliation and retention. FO will prepare a report detailing the unused coupons and consult with the University Administration on their subsequent use.
3.6       No exchange coupons back to cash or accept return from participants.

4          Storage and Security
4.1       To ensure the secure handling of coupons and e-coupons, the following controls must be implemented.
Physical Coupons: Store all physical coupons in locked cabinets or secure storage areas with restricted access. Maintain an up-to-date inventory log that includes the number of coupons, serial numbers, denominations, and the name of the designated custodian responsible for their safekeeping.
E-Coupons:  Store  e-coupon  codes  in  secure,  access-controlled  systems.  Use  encryption where available and ensure that all access and transactions are logged to maintain an audit trail. Under no circumstances should e-coupon codes be stored in unsecured formats such as unencrypted spreadsheets, emails, or paper records.
4.2       To   strengthen   internal    controls,   the   responsibilities    for   purchasing,   custody,   and reconciliation of coupons should be assigned to different individuals whenever practicable.
4.3       A physical or electronic inventory count of all coupons shall be conducted regularly, such as every three months. Any discrepancies must be promptly investigated. Losses or suspected theft must be reported immediately to the PI, department head and FO.



5          Reporting Requirements
5.1       To ensure the secure handling of coupons and e-coupons, the following controls must be implemented.
Financial Reporting: All coupon-related expenditures must be accurately recorded in the University’s   financial   system   under    the    appropriate    account   codes.   Supporting documentation, including purchase receipts, distribution logs, and approval records, must be retained and made available for financial reporting and reconciliation.
Research Fund Reporting: For projects funded by external grants or internal research funds, the use of coupons must be reported in accordance with the terms and conditions of the funding source. This includes specifying the purpose, value, and distribution details of the coupons in financial reports or progress updates, as required.
Audit Readiness: Departments must maintain complete and auditable records of all coupon transactions for a minimum retention period of 7 years, including:
•   Purchase documentation (invoices, approvals)
•   Distribution logs with recipient identifiers
•   Inventory records and reconciliation reports
•   Justifications for use and alignment with project objectives

6          Prohibitions and Restrictions
6.1      To ensure proper use and accountability, the following practices are strictly prohibited.
•   Coupons must not be used to pay vendors, researchers, student helpers, contractors, or service providers for goods or services rendered.
•   The purchase or distribution of reloadable cards or cards that allow cash withdrawals (e.g., prepaid debit cards) is prohibited.
•   Coupons must not be exchanged for cash under any circumstances. Departments must not accept returned coupons from participants.
•   Employees and affiliates are strictly prohibited from using coupons for personal benefit or engaging in resale of any kind. Any  suspected misuse, loss, or error in coupon handling must be reported promptly to Dean or Head of Department for investigation and follow-up.

7          Privacy and Data Protection
7.1      To uphold ethical standards and meet data protection requirements, safeguards must be in place to protect participant privacy when distributing coupons:
•   Collect only the minimum personally identifiable information (PII) needed to administer the coupons.
•    Store all data securely, restrict access to authorized personnel, and use it solely for the stated purpose.



•   Keep participant identities anonymous or confidential wherever feasible.
•   Protect both physical and digital records from unauthorized access, loss, or misuse.
•   Ensure all procedures comply with the University’s data protection policies and all applicable laws.
